"""'In the interview' answers, part 2: a likely interviewer question for each remaining concept, with the answer to give."""

def q(question, answer):
    return [("Interviewer: " + question, answer)]

SAY2 = {
"client-server": q("Why not let clients talk to the database directly?",
  "\"Clients are untrusted and numerous. A server layer lets us authenticate and authorise every request, validate input, hide the schema, cache, rate-limit and change the database without shipping new apps. Direct DB access would expose credentials and make scaling and security impossible.\""),
"cap-intro": q("Your service runs in two regions and the link between them breaks. What happens?",
  "\"That's a partition, so for each feature I choose. For the account balance we stay consistent: the region that can't reach the primary rejects writes until the link heals. For the feed and likes we stay available: both regions keep serving possibly stale data and reconcile afterwards.\""),
"ip-dns": q("How would you use DNS for failover between two regions?",
  "\"Use health-checked DNS records with a short TTL, 30–60 seconds: GeoDNS or latency-based routing sends users to the nearest healthy region, and if health checks fail, the record switches to the other region. Because some resolvers ignore TTLs, I'd pair it with Anycast or a global load balancer for faster failover.\""),
"tcp-udp": q("Why does video calling use UDP?",
  "\"For real-time media, a packet that arrives late is useless. TCP would retransmit and stall everything behind the lost packet, so a 1% loss causes freezes. Over UDP (WebRTC/RTP) we simply skip the lost packet, conceal it, and adapt bitrate — lower latency matters more than perfect delivery.\""),
"tls": q("Where would you terminate TLS?",
  "\"At the edge load balancer or API gateway, so certificates are managed in one place and backends are offloaded. Inside the network I'd still encrypt service-to-service traffic with mTLS through the service mesh — zero trust — and for sensitive paths like payments keep end-to-end TLS.\""),
"graphql": q("Would you expose GraphQL or REST for the mobile app?",
  "\"For a mobile app with many screens pulling data from several services, GraphQL as a BFF works well: one round trip, exactly the fields each screen needs. I'd guard it with query depth/complexity limits, persisted queries, and DataLoader batching to avoid N+1 calls. Public partner APIs I'd keep as REST because they're simpler to cache and document.\""),
"realtime-comm": q("How would you push live updates to a million connected users?",
  "\"Clients hold WebSocket connections to a fleet of gateway servers, each handling ~50–100k connections. A Redis registry maps user → gateway. When an event happens, the backend publishes it to a pub/sub channel; the gateway holding that user pushes it. Heartbeats detect dead connections, and clients reconnect with backoff to any gateway.\""),
"api-gateway": q("What would your API gateway do, and how do you keep it from being a bottleneck?",
  "\"It terminates TLS, authenticates tokens, applies rate limits, routes by path to services and adds request IDs for tracing. It holds no business logic and no state, so it scales horizontally behind an L4 load balancer across zones, and it's the first thing we load-test.\""),
"proxies": q("Where do reverse proxies appear in your design?",
  "\"The CDN and the load balancer are both reverse proxies: they sit in front of our servers, hide their addresses, terminate TLS, cache static responses and absorb attacks. Internally, Envoy sidecars act as reverse proxies for each service in the mesh.\""),
"load-balancer": q("What happens if the load balancer itself fails?",
  "\"We never run one. Load balancers run as a redundant pair or a managed multi-AZ service with health-checked nodes; for self-managed ones, keepalived/VRRP moves a virtual IP to the standby within seconds. At global scale DNS or Anycast spreads traffic across several LB clusters, so losing one cluster just shifts traffic.\""),
"lb-algorithms": q("Which load-balancing algorithm would you choose here and why?",
  "\"Requests are short and uniform, so round robin is fine for the API tier. For the WebSocket tier connections are long-lived and uneven, so least-connections. For the cache tier I need the same key to hit the same node, so consistent hashing.\""),
"cdn": q("How do you make sure users get the new JavaScript after a deploy?",
  "\"Every build produces content-hashed filenames like app.3f9a2c.js, served with a one-year Cache-Control. The HTML that references them has a short TTL. So a deploy changes the filenames, browsers and CDN fetch the new files immediately, and we never need to purge.\""),
"caching": q("What would you cache in this system, and where?",
  "\"Static assets and images on the CDN; hot read data — user profiles, product pages, feed IDs — in Redis with TTLs; tiny, very hot config in an in-process cache. I'd aim for a 90%+ hit ratio on the read path and monitor it, keeping the database as the source of truth.\""),
"cache-eviction": q("Your Redis is full. What gets evicted?",
  "\"I'd configure allkeys-lru so the least recently used keys go first, which suits general caching. If a stable set of items is always popular, allkeys-lfu keeps frequently used keys longer. Every key also has a TTL so stale data leaves on its own.\""),
"redis-memcached": q("Redis or Memcached for this cache?",
  "\"Redis, because besides plain caching we need sorted sets for the leaderboard, atomic counters and Lua for rate limiting, and replication with automatic failover. Memcached would only win for a huge, simple multithreaded key-value cache with no persistence needs.\""),
"message-queues": q("Why put a queue between the order service and the email service?",
  "\"So placing an order doesn't depend on the email provider being fast or up. The order service publishes OrderPlaced and returns; email workers consume at their own pace with retries, and after repeated failures the message goes to a dead-letter queue we alert on. The queue also absorbs sale-day spikes.\""),
"kafka": q("How do you preserve ordering of events for one order in Kafka?",
  "\"Use the order ID as the message key, so all events for that order land in the same partition, which Kafka keeps in order. Within a consumer group each partition is read by one consumer, so the order is processed sequentially. I'd enable the idempotent producer and acks=all so retries don't duplicate or reorder.\""),
"backpressure": q("Producers suddenly send 10× more events than consumers can handle. What happens?",
  "\"Kafka just buffers it — consumer lag grows but nothing is lost. We alert on lag and autoscale consumers up to the partition count. At the API edge, if internal queues are full, we shed low-priority traffic with 503 and Retry-After rather than letting latency explode for everyone.\""),
"stream-batch": q("Should this analytics pipeline be batch or streaming?",
  "\"Both. Real-time counts for the dashboard come from a Flink job over Kafka with one-minute windows and watermarks for late events. The authoritative daily numbers come from a Spark batch job over the raw data in S3, which can reprocess history. If they disagree, batch wins.\""),
"scheduler-cron": q("How do you make sure a scheduled job runs exactly once across 10 servers?",
  "\"Jobs live in a database. Each due run is claimed with a unique (job_id, scheduled_time) row — or a lease — so only one worker inserts it successfully. The worker heartbeats its lease; if it dies, the lease expires and another worker retries. The job itself is idempotent so a retry after a partial run is safe.\""),
"storage-types": q("Where do user-uploaded photos live?",
  "\"In object storage like S3: the client gets a pre-signed URL and uploads directly, so bytes never pass through our servers. The database stores only metadata — owner, S3 key, size, checksum. Photos are served through a CDN, and lifecycle rules move old originals to cheaper storage.\""),
"search-engines": q("How do you add full-text search to this product?",
  "\"The database stays the source of truth. Changes flow via CDC or events into Elasticsearch, which builds an inverted index sharded and replicated across nodes. Search queries hit Elasticsearch, get IDs ranked by BM25 plus popularity, then hydrate details from cache. The index is eventually consistent, typically a second behind.\""),
"timeseries-analytics": q("Product wants reports like revenue by city by month. Run them on the main DB?",
  "\"No — heavy analytical scans would hurt the OLTP database. I'd stream changes via CDC into a columnar warehouse like BigQuery, Snowflake or ClickHouse and run reports there, where scanning billions of rows on a few columns is cheap.\""),
"acid": q("Explain how a money transfer stays correct if the server crashes halfway.",
  "\"Both updates run in one transaction. Atomicity means a crash before COMMIT rolls back the debit, using the undo log. Durability means once COMMIT returns, the write-ahead log is on disk and survives a crash. Isolation means a concurrent reader never sees money that left A but hasn't reached B. And constraints like balance ≥ 0 keep the data consistent.\""),
"indexes": q("This query is slow: WHERE user_id = ? AND created_at > ? ORDER BY created_at DESC. What index?",
  "\"A composite index on (user_id, created_at DESC). The leftmost column matches the equality filter, the second gives the range and the sort order, so the DB reads just the needed slice in order. If the query only needs a few columns, I'd make it a covering index to avoid touching the table at all.\""),
"storage-engines": q("Why does Cassandra handle writes so well?",
  "\"It's built on an LSM tree: a write is appended to a commit log and inserted into an in-memory memtable — both sequential and cheap — then flushed as immutable sorted SSTables and merged by background compaction. Reads may check several SSTables, so it uses Bloom filters to skip files that can't contain the key.\""),
"nosql-types": q("How would you model chat messages in Cassandra?",
  "\"Table messages with partition key (conversation_id, month_bucket) and clustering key message_time descending. 'Last 50 messages of a chat' is one sequential read of a single partition, and the month bucket keeps partitions from growing without bound.\""),
"normalization": q("Would you store the author's name inside each post?",
  "\"For the feed, yes — denormalising the display name and avatar into the post read model avoids a join on every feed load. The users table stays the source of truth, and when someone renames, an event updates the copies asynchronously; a few seconds of staleness is fine.\""),
"replication": q("A user updates their profile and immediately sees the old one. Why, and how do you fix it?",
  "\"The write went to the primary but the read hit an async replica that hadn't caught up — replication lag. I'd route a user's reads of their own data to the primary for a short window after they write (or pin the session to the primary), while everyone else keeps reading from replicas.\""),
"sharding": q("How would you shard the users and posts data?",
  "\"Shard by user_id using consistent hashing, so a user's profile and posts live on the same shard and most queries hit one shard. I'd pre-split into many logical shards — say 4,096 — mapped onto fewer physical databases, so we rebalance by moving whole logical shards. Global lookups like 'find by email' use a separate index table.\""),
"consistent-hashing": q("Why not just use hash(key) % N to pick a cache node?",
  "\"Because changing N — adding or losing a node — remaps almost every key, and the cache hit rate collapses. With consistent hashing, nodes and keys sit on a hash ring and each key goes to the next node clockwise, so adding a node only moves about 1/N of the keys. Virtual nodes, 100–200 per server, keep the load even.\""),
"db-scaling-path": q("Your single Postgres is at 80% CPU. What do you do, in order?",
  "\"First find the expensive queries and fix indexes and N+1 patterns, and add a connection pooler. Then cache hot reads in Redis and move reads to replicas. Then scale the machine up. If writes are still the limit, split by feature into separate databases, and finally shard the biggest tables by user ID.\""),
"connection-pooling": q("You have 300 app servers and Postgres allows 500 connections. Problem?",
  "\"Yes — even 5 connections per server is 1,500. I'd put PgBouncer or RDS Proxy in transaction-pooling mode in front, so thousands of client connections share ~100–200 real ones, and keep each app's pool small. Databases perform worse with too many active connections anyway.\""),
"distributed-transactions": q("An order needs inventory, payment and shipping services to all succeed. How?",
  "\"A saga orchestrated by the order service: reserve inventory, then charge payment, then create the shipment, each a local transaction with an idempotent API. If payment fails, run compensations — release the inventory and cancel the order. Each step emits events through an outbox so nothing is lost, and the order shows intermediate states like PAYMENT_PENDING.\""),
"cdc": q("How do you keep Elasticsearch in sync with the database?",
  "\"Not by writing to both from the application — that dual write can fail halfway. I'd use change data capture: Debezium tails the database's write-ahead log, publishes each change to Kafka, and an indexer consumes it and updates Elasticsearch. Ordering per key is preserved, and we can replay from Kafka to rebuild the index.\""),
"blob-metadata": q("Where do you store a 50 MB PDF a user uploads?",
  "\"The PDF goes to object storage via a pre-signed upload URL; the database keeps a small row — file ID, owner, S3 key, size, content type, checksum. Downloads go through a CDN or a short-lived signed URL. Thumbnails and virus scans run asynchronously off the upload event.\""),
"cap": q("Is your design CP or AP?",
  "\"Per feature. The payment ledger and inventory are CP — a node that can't reach a quorum rejects writes rather than risk inconsistency. The product catalogue, reviews and recommendations are AP — they keep serving from local replicas during a partition and converge afterwards.\""),
"pacelc": q("Even without failures, why not make everything strongly consistent?",
  "\"Because consistency costs latency even when nothing is broken: a synchronous write has to wait for replicas, which across regions adds 100+ ms. So for user-facing reads like feeds I take low latency with eventual consistency, and pay the latency only where correctness requires it — that's the 'else' part of PACELC.\""),
"consistency-models": q("What consistency do users need for comments on a post?",
  "\"At least causal plus read-your-writes: a user must see their own comment immediately, and replies must never appear before the comment they reply to. Global strong consistency isn't needed — other users can see new comments a second later.\""),
"quorum": q("With 3 replicas, how do you configure reads and writes?",
  "\"For strong-ish consistency, write quorum W = 2 and read quorum R = 2, so R + W > N and every read overlaps the latest write; it tolerates one replica down. For a write-heavy, staleness-tolerant workload I'd use W = 1, R = 1 and rely on read repair and anti-entropy.\""),
"time-clocks": q("Can you use server timestamps to decide which of two concurrent writes wins?",
  "\"Not safely. Clocks across machines drift by milliseconds or more and NTP can jump, so last-write-wins can silently drop the truly later write. If order matters I'd route writes for a key through a single leader that assigns sequence numbers, or use version vectors to detect concurrent writes and merge them.\""),
"vector-clocks": q("Two users edit the same shopping cart offline. How do you merge?",
  "\"Each replica tracks a version vector. When the vectors show the two versions are concurrent, we keep both as siblings and merge with a domain rule — for a cart, the union of items, which is what Amazon's Dynamo did. A deleted item that reappears is the trade-off, fixed with an OR-set CRDT.\""),
"consensus": q("How does Raft keep a replicated log consistent?",
  "\"One leader per term handles all writes. It appends the entry, replicates it to followers, and commits once a majority has stored it. If the leader dies, followers time out and elect a new one, and a node only votes for candidates whose log is at least as up to date, so committed entries are never lost. Five nodes tolerate two failures.\""),
"leader-election": q("Only one instance should run the billing job. How do you ensure that?",
  "\"Use leader election in etcd or ZooKeeper: instances compete for a lease key; the holder is leader and renews the lease every few seconds. If it dies, the lease expires and another takes over. Writes from the job carry a fencing token from the lease so a paused former leader can't corrupt anything.\""),
"distributed-locks": q("Is a Redis SETNX lock safe for preventing double processing?",
  "\"For avoiding duplicate work, yes: SET key value NX PX 30000 with a random value, released by a compare-and-delete Lua script. For correctness it isn't enough — a holder paused past expiry can still write. Then I'd use fencing tokens from a consensus-backed lock service, or avoid locks with a unique constraint or a conditional update.\""),
"gossip": q("How does a 1,000-node cluster know which nodes are alive without a central server?",
  "\"Gossip. Every second each node exchanges its membership table and heartbeat counters with a few random peers, so information reaches everyone in O(log N) rounds. A phi-accrual detector marks a node suspect when its heartbeats stop increasing, which avoids false alarms from brief network blips.\""),
"merkle-anti-entropy": q("How do two replicas find which keys differ without comparing everything?",
  "\"Each replica builds a Merkle tree over its key ranges: leaves hash small ranges, parents hash their children. They compare root hashes; if they match, they're in sync. If not, they descend only into the differing branches, so finding differences costs O(log n) hash comparisons, then they stream just those ranges.\""),
"crdt": q("How would you keep a 'like' counter correct in two active regions?",
  "\"Use a PN-counter CRDT: each region counts its own increments and decrements, the value is the sum, and merging takes the per-region maximum. Regions accept likes independently with no coordination, and replicas always converge to the same total.\""),
"unique-id": q("How do you generate IDs for tweets across hundreds of servers?",
  "\"Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs: 41 bits of milliseconds since a custom epoch, 10 bits of machine ID leased from ZooKeeper, and a 12-bit sequence. They're unique without coordination, roughly time-sorted — great for 'newest first' — and give 4,096 IDs per millisecond per machine. If the clock goes backwards, the generator waits or refuses.\""),
"bloom-filter": q("Your crawler has seen 5 billion URLs. How do you check 'seen before' cheaply?",
  "\"A Bloom filter: with about 10 bits per URL, roughly 6 GB, it answers 'definitely new' or 'probably seen' with ~1% false positives and no false negatives. A false positive just means we occasionally skip a new URL, which is acceptable. For exactness on the positives we can confirm in a key-value store.\""),
"hyperloglog": q("How do you count unique daily visitors for 10 million pages?",
  "\"A HyperLogLog per page per day — Redis PFADD and PFCOUNT — uses about 12 KB each regardless of traffic, with ~1% error. They merge with PFMERGE, so weekly uniques are the merge of daily ones. Exact sets of user IDs would cost gigabytes.\""),
"geohash-quadtree": q("How do you find drivers within 2 km of a rider quickly?",
  "\"Keep live driver locations in an in-memory geo index — geohash cells in Redis GEO, or H3 cells — updated every few seconds. For a request, compute the rider's cell at a precision near 2 km, fetch drivers in that cell and its 8 neighbours, filter by exact distance and availability, then rank by ETA.\""),
"trie-structures": q("How do you return autocomplete suggestions in under 50 ms?",
  "\"A trie built offline from query logs, where every node stores its precomputed top 10 completions. A lookup just walks the prefix — O(prefix length) — and returns that list. The trie is sharded by prefix, kept in memory, and refreshed periodically, with popular prefixes cached at the CDN.\""),
"monolith-microservices": q("Would you start this product as microservices?",
  "\"No — for a new product with one team I'd build a modular monolith with clear module boundaries and separate schemas per module. It's faster to build and debug. When teams grow or one module needs to scale or deploy independently — say search or payments — I'd extract it along that boundary.\""),
"service-discovery": q("How does the order service find the payment service's instances?",
  "\"In Kubernetes, through a Service: a stable DNS name like payments.svc.cluster.local backed by a virtual IP that load-balances across healthy pods, which readiness probes keep up to date. Outside Kubernetes I'd use Consul or Eureka, with instances registering and sending heartbeats.\""),
"service-mesh": q("Would you add a service mesh?",
  "\"At dozens of services, yes. Envoy sidecars give us mTLS between services, consistent retries, timeouts and circuit breaking, canary traffic splits and uniform metrics and traces without changing code. For five services it's overhead, and a good client library is enough.\""),
"bff": q("Web and mobile need different data for the same screen. How do you serve both?",
  "\"A backend-for-frontend per client: a mobile BFF and a web BFF that call the domain services in parallel and shape the response for that client — smaller payloads for mobile. Business rules stay in the domain services; BFFs only aggregate and format.\""),
"event-driven": q("Why publish an OrderPlaced event instead of calling each service?",
  "\"The order service shouldn't need to know who cares about orders. It publishes OrderPlaced once; inventory, email, analytics and loyalty subscribe independently, and adding a new consumer needs no change to the order service. The cost is eventual consistency and harder tracing, so we use correlation IDs and an event schema registry.\""),
"event-sourcing": q("Would you use event sourcing for the wallet?",
  "\"Yes — a wallet is naturally a sequence of events like TopUp, Payment and Refund. Storing them append-only gives a complete audit trail and lets us rebuild balances or answer 'what was the balance on 1 March'. We'd snapshot balances periodically so replay is fast, and project read models for the history screen.\""),
"cqrs": q("Reads vastly outnumber writes and need different shapes. How do you structure that?",
  "\"CQRS: commands go to a normalised write model in Postgres; every change emits an event that updates read models — a denormalised feed table, Elasticsearch for search, Redis counters. Reads hit the read models and scale independently, accepting that they're a second behind the write side.\""),
"serverless-containers": q("Lambda or Kubernetes for the image-resizing service?",
  "\"Lambda: resizing is event-driven — triggered by an upload — spiky, short-lived and stateless, so pay-per-use and automatic scaling fit perfectly. The core API with steady traffic and latency targets I'd run as containers on Kubernetes, where cold starts and per-invocation costs don't hurt.\""),
"resilience-patterns": q("The recommendations service is slow. How do you keep the home page fast?",
  "\"Call it with a tight timeout, say 150 ms, behind a circuit breaker and its own bulkhead thread pool, so it can't exhaust threads the rest of the page needs. If it times out or the breaker is open, fall back to cached or generic popular items. The page degrades gracefully instead of failing.\""),
"idempotency": q("The client's 'place order' request timed out. Can it just retry?",
  "\"Yes, safely, because every create request carries an Idempotency-Key. The server stores the key with a unique constraint before doing the work; a retry with the same key returns the original result instead of creating a second order. Keys expire after 24 hours.\""),
"deployments": q("How do you release a risky change without an outage?",
  "\"Ship it behind a feature flag, deploy with a canary — 1%, then 5%, 25%, 100% — while watching error rate and p99 against the baseline, with automatic rollback if they regress. Database changes use expand–contract so old and new code both work during the rollout.\""),
"config-multitenancy": q("How do you isolate tenants in a SaaS product?",
  "\"Most tenants share tables with a tenant_id column, enforced by row-level security and in every query path, with per-tenant rate limits so one noisy tenant can't starve others. Large enterprise tenants can be moved to dedicated databases. Data for EU customers stays in an EU region for GDPR.\""),
"redundancy-failover": q("How does your system survive losing an entire availability zone?",
  "\"Every tier runs in at least three AZs: stateless services spread across zones behind the load balancer, the database with a synchronous standby in another AZ, Kafka and Redis with replicas across zones. Capacity is planned so two zones can carry peak load. AZ loss is automatic failover with no data loss.\""),
"disaster-recovery": q("What's your disaster recovery plan?",
  "\"It depends on RPO and RTO. For the core service I'd target RPO under a minute and RTO under 15 minutes: continuous async replication to a warm standby region, infrastructure as code to scale it up, DNS failover, and quarterly DR drills. Plus point-in-time backups with immutable copies against accidental deletes and ransomware.\""),
"monitoring": q("How would you know your system is unhealthy before users complain?",
  "\"Dashboards and alerts on the golden signals — latency p99, error rate, traffic and saturation — per service, tied to SLOs with burn-rate alerts. Centralised structured logs with request IDs and distributed tracing with OpenTelemetry let us pinpoint which hop is slow. Synthetic checks probe critical flows like login and checkout every minute.\""),
"autoscaling": q("Traffic is 5× higher every evening and on sale days. How do you handle it?",
  "\"The stateless tiers autoscale on request rate or CPU with a target of ~60% utilisation. For known peaks like sale days we scale up on a schedule beforehand, because reactive scaling lags by minutes, and pre-warm caches. Databases don't autoscale quickly, so they're sized for peak with read replicas.\""),
"chaos-engineering": q("How do you know your failover actually works?",
  "\"We test it regularly: game days where we kill a database primary or block an AZ in staging and then carefully in production, plus automated chaos experiments that terminate random instances. Each has a hypothesis — 'p99 stays under 300 ms' — and an abort switch.\""),
"cost": q("This design looks expensive. Where would you save money?",
  "\"The big costs are usually egress, storage and over-provisioned compute. I'd serve media through a CDN to cut origin egress, tier old data to cheaper storage classes, use spot instances for batch jobs and reserved capacity for steady load, right-size instances from utilisation data, and set retention limits on logs.\""),
"authn-authz": q("How do you make sure a user can only see their own orders?",
  "\"Authentication gives us a verified user ID from the token. Authorisation then runs on the server for every request: the order query always includes owner_id = current user, or an access check confirms the relationship. We never trust an ID from the client alone — that prevents insecure direct object reference bugs.\""),
"sessions-jwt": q("Sessions or JWTs for your API?",
  "\"JWT access tokens valid for 10–15 minutes, signed with RS256, so every service can verify them locally without a session lookup. Plus a long-lived refresh token stored securely and rotated on use, which we can revoke server-side. Short access lifetimes limit the damage of a leaked token.\""),
"oauth": q("How does 'Sign in with Google' work?",
  "\"It's OpenID Connect on OAuth 2.0 with the authorization code flow and PKCE. We redirect the user to Google with our client ID and scopes; after they log in, Google redirects back with a one-time code. Our backend exchanges the code for an ID token, a JWT saying who the user is, which we verify, then create our own session.\""),
"encryption": q("How do you protect sensitive data at rest?",
  "\"Encrypt disks, databases and S3 objects with AES-256 using envelope encryption: data keys encrypt the data, and a master key in KMS encrypts the data keys, so rotation is cheap and access to keys is audited. Highly sensitive fields like PAN or Aadhaar get field-level encryption or tokenisation, and secrets live in a secrets manager, never in code.\""),
"web-security": q("What security measures would you put in this design?",
  "\"Defence in depth: CDN and WAF at the edge for DDoS and common attacks; TLS everywhere; the gateway authenticates and rate-limits; services validate input and use parameterised queries; authorisation checks on every resource; least-privilege IAM; databases in private subnets; encryption at rest; and audit logs.\""),
"feed-fanout": q("How do you build each user's home feed?",
  "\"A hybrid fan-out. When a normal user posts, a worker pushes the post ID into each active follower's feed list in Redis — fan-out on write — so reading is one fast lookup. Celebrities with millions of followers are not fanned out; their recent posts are merged in at read time. Then we rank and hydrate the post details from cache.\""),
"counters": q("How do you count likes on a post that gets 50,000 likes a second?",
  "\"Not with a single row update — that row becomes a hot spot. Store each like as an event, aggregate in a stream job or increment sharded counters in Redis (16 sub-counters summed on read), and flush totals to the database every few seconds. The UI shows an approximate '1.2M', which is fine.\""),
"notification-pattern": q("How do you make sure an OTP SMS goes out within seconds even during a marketing blast?",
  "\"Separate queues by priority: OTPs and security alerts go to a high-priority queue with dedicated workers, so the marketing backlog never delays them. Workers call the SMS provider with retries, and fail over to a second provider if the first errors or exceeds a latency threshold.\""),
"chat-delivery": q("How do you guarantee messages arrive in order and are never lost?",
  "\"The server persists every message before acknowledging 'sent', and assigns a per-conversation sequence number from that conversation's partition owner. Recipients ack delivery; if they were offline, on reconnect they ask for everything after their last sequence number, so gaps are filled and order is by sequence, not timestamps. Client message IDs dedupe retries.\""),
"video-streaming": q("How does a video play smoothly on a slow mobile connection?",
  "\"Each video is transcoded into several bitrates and cut into 2–6 second segments listed in an HLS or DASH manifest. The player measures throughput and buffer and picks the quality per segment — dropping to 360p on a weak network instead of stalling. Segments come from the nearest CDN edge.\""),
"file-sync": q("A user edits one page of a 1 GB file. What gets uploaded?",
  "\"Only the changed chunks. Files are split with content-defined chunking into ~4 MB blocks identified by their SHA-256 hash; the client asks which hashes the server lacks and uploads just those, then commits a new block list for the file version. Unchanged blocks are deduplicated, even across users.\""),
"payments-ledger": q("How do you make sure a payment never charges twice?",
  "\"Every payment request carries an idempotency key stored with a unique constraint, so retries return the original result. We pass our payment ID to the provider as their idempotency key too. The payment moves through a state machine with conditional updates, and money movements are double-entry ledger rows. Unknown outcomes are resolved by querying the provider and by daily reconciliation.\""),
"inventory-overselling": q("1 lakh users try to buy 1,000 phones at noon. How do you avoid overselling?",
  "\"Load the stock into Redis and decrement atomically with a Lua script that refuses to go below zero, and give each winner a 10-minute reservation persisted asynchronously to the database. A virtual waiting room admits users at a rate the system can handle, and once stock hits zero everyone else gets 'sold out' instantly from the cache.\""),
"leaderboard-pattern": q("How do you show a live top-10 and each player's rank?",
  "\"A Redis sorted set: ZINCRBY on every score update, ZREVRANGE 0 9 for the top 10, ZREVRANK for a player's rank — all O(log n). Scores are also persisted to a database so the set can be rebuilt. Weekly boards are separate keys with TTLs.\""),
"crawler-politeness": q("How do you make sure your crawler doesn't overload a website?",
  "\"The frontier keeps a separate queue per host, and each host is assigned to one worker that fetches with a delay between requests, honouring robots.txt and crawl-delay. So no matter how many URLs we have for a site, it only sees one polite connection at a time.\""),
"recsys-ml": q("How would you design 'recommended for you' at scale?",
  "\"A two-stage funnel. Candidate generation narrows millions of items to a few hundred using embeddings with approximate nearest-neighbour search plus trending and 'because you watched' sources. A ranking model scores those with user, item and context features from a feature store, and a final re-ranking step adds diversity and business rules. Results are cached per user and refreshed regularly.\""),
"llm-systems": q("Design a chatbot that answers questions from our internal documents.",
  "\"RAG. Offline, split documents into chunks, embed them and store vectors with metadata and access-control tags in a vector database. At query time, embed the question, retrieve the top chunks the user is allowed to see, re-rank, and put them in the prompt so the LLM answers with citations. Stream the answer over SSE, cache frequent answers, and evaluate quality continuously.\""),
"read-write-heavy": q("Is this system read-heavy or write-heavy, and how does that change your design?",
  "\"From the estimate it's about 100:1 read-heavy, so the design centres on serving reads cheaply: CDN, Redis cache with a high hit ratio, read replicas and precomputed views. Writes are modest and can go to a single primary per shard, with async processing for anything slow.\""),
"hotspots": q("One celebrity's profile is getting 1 million reads per second. What breaks and how do you fix it?",
  "\"The single cache shard holding that key melts. I'd detect the hot key from metrics, then serve it from an in-process cache on every app server with a short TTL, and replicate it under several keys spread across shards. For writes to a hot counter, split it into sub-counters and aggregate.\""),
"uml": q("Can you sketch the classes and their relationships?",
  "\"ParkingLot composes Floors — floors don't exist without the lot. Floor aggregates Spots. CarSpot and BikeSpot extend Spot. Ticket has an association with a Vehicle and a Spot. ParkingLot depends on a FeeStrategy interface, realised by HourlyFee and FlatFee. Multiplicity: one lot, one or more floors, many spots per floor.\""),
"creational-patterns": q("When would you use a Builder instead of a constructor?",
  "\"When an object has many optional parameters — like a Pizza or an HTTP request. A Builder gives readable, named setters, validates in build(), and produces an immutable object, avoiding telescoping constructors where nobody remembers what the fifth boolean means.\""),
"structural-patterns": q("How would you add logging and caching to a repository without changing it?",
  "\"Decorators: CachingRepository and LoggingRepository both implement the same Repository interface and wrap another Repository, adding their behaviour before or after delegating. They can be stacked in any order, and the real repository stays untouched.\""),
"behavioral-patterns": q("Which pattern fits a vending machine and why?",
  "\"The State pattern. The machine's behaviour depends on its state — Idle, HasSelection, Dispensing — so each state is a class that handles the actions valid in it and moves to the next state. Adding an Admin or OutOfService state means adding a class instead of editing a big switch statement.\""),
"concurrency-java": q("Two threads book the last seat at the same time. How do you prevent double booking in Java?",
  "\"Make the claim atomic per seat: use ConcurrentHashMap.putIfAbsent(seatId, bookingId) — only one thread succeeds — or lock per show with synchronized/ReentrantLock rather than one global lock, so different shows book in parallel. In a distributed setup the same idea becomes a conditional database update.\""),
"api-contract-lld": q("How do you structure the code inside a service?",
  "\"Controller → service → repository. Controllers handle HTTP and validation, services hold business rules and depend on repository interfaces, repositories handle persistence. Domain objects are immutable records with enums for fixed sets, money as long paise, Optional for not-found, and constructor injection so everything is testable.\""),
}
